    "markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Model composition and structured populations\"\ncourse-unit: a07-model-composition\nstatus: complete\n---\n\n## Why compose models?\n\n- Named components expose biological assumptions and connections.\n- One Catalyst reaction network can generate deterministic and stochastic\n  problem types.\n- Structure matters: homogeneous mixing hides group-specific contact patterns.\n\nToday: current ModelingToolkit 11 and Catalyst 16 APIs.\n\n::: {.notes}\nComposition is an audit tool. Compile and inspect the equations before trusting\nthe numerical trajectory.\n:::\n\n## Symbolic components\n\nFor a deterministic continuous-time continuous-state SIR:\n\n$$\n\\dot S=-\\beta SI/N,\\qquad\n\\dot I=\\beta SI/N-\\gamma I,\\qquad\n\\dot R=\\gamma I.\n$$\n\nKeep the flows in separate `System` components, connect shared states and\nparameters, then call `compose` and `mtkcompile`.\n\n\n\n\n::: {.notes}\nThe complete connection system is shown in the book and notebook. The key API\nis the current `System` → `compose` → `mtkcompile` pipeline.\n:::\n\n## Structured mixing as reactions\n\nFor two groups, $m_{12}=m_{21}=0.5$ modifies cross-group infection:\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=\\frac{\\beta c_1}{N}(S_1I_1+m_{12}S_1I_2),\\quad\n\\lambda_2=\\frac{\\beta c_2}{N}(m_{21}S_2I_1+S_2I_2).\n$$\n\n\n\n\n## One network, multiple interpretations\n\n\n\n\nThe same `ReactionSystem` can be passed to an `SDEProblem` (chemical-Langevin\napproximation) or `JumpProblem` (integer-count event process). Their state\nspaces and error claims are different.\n\n## Generated comparison\n\n::: {#8 .cell execution_count=1}\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```{=html}\n<img width=800 height=350 style='object-fit: contain; height: auto;' src=\"data:image/png;base64, \"/>\n```\n\nAggregate infectious prevalence generated from the structured reaction-network model.\n:::\n:::\n\n\n\nValidation: the ODE and jump stoichiometry conserve\n$S_1+I_1+R_1+S_2+I_2+R_2$. A Gaussian SDE can leave the nonnegative domain;\nreport that diagnostic rather than clipping it silently.\n\n## Takeaways and links\n\n- Use `System`, `compose`, and `mtkcompile` for inspectable symbolic assembly.\n- Use Catalyst reactions to preserve stoichiometry and reuse a model.\n- Group structure introduces parameters that aggregate incidence may not identify.\n\nRead the [book appendix](../../appendices/07-model-composition.html),\n[ModelingToolkit composition](https://docs.sciml.ai/ModelingToolkit/stable/basics/Composition/),\nand [Catalyst system generation](https://catalyst.sciml.ai/stable/manual/systems/).\n\n::: {.notes}\nDo not equate ODE, SDE, and jump trajectories. They are related model\ninterpretations with different state spaces, noise, and validation requirements.\n:::\n\n",
